# Summary
Introduced. The commit added CreateStreamUpload metadata commands and calls set_pending_metadata_command_for_bucket from stream creation paths without bucket-level serialization. Existing pending-command storage only debug-asserts on overwrite, making the newly unguarded paths capable of losing retry state under concurrent streaming PUTs and a partial metadata apply failure.
This commit moves PutObject stream-upload session creation onto the replicated metadata-command path. However, both create_put_object_stream_session_record and create_put_object_stream_session set a pending metadata command directly after reserve_put_object_generation returns, without holding the same bucket lock used by other object metadata command flows. The pending-command map is keyed only by (PG, bucket), and set_pending_metadata_command_for_bucket uses HashMap::insert with only a debug_assert when a previous command exists. In release builds, a concurrent object operation can therefore overwrite a stream-create pending command, or a stream-create can overwrite another pending command. This is especially dangerous because partial metadata-command application relies on the pending command remaining present for later retry; apply_pending_object_metadata_command_for_bucket intentionally leaves a failed partially-applied command pending. If the pending entry is overwritten/removed during a race, replicas can be left permanently inconsistent, reservations can leak, or subsequent writes/aborts/finalization can fail or operate on divergent stream-upload metadata.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `1cc2543` (`Prevent pending metadata command overwrite`).

The fix replaces release-build overwriting of the per-`(PG,bucket)` pending metadata command slot with a checked insert that returns `PendingMetadataCommandConflict` and preserves the existing pending command. All metadata-command publishers now go through the checked `StorageCluster` helper, so bucket commands, object commands, stream-create, stream append/abort, multipart create/complete/abort, and direct PUT cannot silently discard another pending convergence record.

The stream-create path now handles a pending-slot collision by draining the existing pending object metadata command and releasing the losing generation reservation before retrying or returning an explicit error. Direct PUT cleanup paths also drain before releasing a generation reservation, so cleanup does not get blocked by the conflicting pending slot.

